1
00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:06.072
Welcome to Evaluating AI Agents
built in partnership with Arize AI.

2
00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:09.342
Say you're building an AI
coding agent, you might have to carry out

3
00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:14.147
a lot of steps to generate good code such
as plan, use tools, reflect, and so on.

4
00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:18.685
And using an evaluation driven development
process would make your development

5
00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:19.919
much more efficient.

6
00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:20.787
In this course,

7
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.657
you'll learn how to add observability
to your agent-based applications. Said this,

8
00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:29.396
You will see what it is doing
every step of the way, so you can evaluate it

9
00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:33.700
components wise and efficiently
drive improvements at the components.

10
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.136
And then also at the whole system level.

11
00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:39.172
So if you're asking yourself questions
like should

12
00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.041
you update a prompt at the last step,

13
00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.410
or should you update
the logic of the workflow,

14
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.413
or should you change the large language
model you're using?

15
00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:50.583
Having a disciplined, evaluation
driven process will help you a lot

16
00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:53.887
in terms of making these decisions
in a systematic rather than random

17
00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.823
try a lot of things
and see what works, kind of way.

18
00:00:56.823 --> 00:01:00.060
If you've heard of the idea of error
analysis, which is a key

19
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.096
concept to machine learning,
this teaches you how to do that

20
00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:06.199
in the agentic workflow
development process.

21
00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.069
If you haven't heard of error analysis,
that's fine too.

22
00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:13.273
But in this course it takes
an important set of ideas and shows you

23
00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:16.409
how to do it to develop agentic workflows
efficiently.

24
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:20.947
The instructors of this course
are John Gilhuly, whose head of developer

25
00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:25.518
relations and Aman Khan,
who is director of product at Arize AI.

26
00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:28.054
It's been fun working with you
on this course.

27
00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.190
Thank you. Andrew.
We're excited to teach this course.

28
00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.291
Thank you.

29
00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:35.695
Say you're building a research agent
that searches the web, identifies sources,

30
00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:39.566
collects content, summarizes findings,
and then maybe iterates

31
00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:42.569
if it identifies
any weaknesses as an output.

32
00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:46.206
When you're building this complex system,
you need to evaluate the

33
00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.575
quality of each step's output.

34
00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:52.445
For example, for source selection,
you might create a test set that comprises

35
00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:56.082
research topics and the corresponding
set of expected sources,

36
00:01:56.449 --> 00:02:00.286
and then measure the percentage of times
that the agent chooses

37
00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.524
to correct sources. Or for open
ended tasks like summarization,

38
00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:08.294
you can prompt a separate
large language model

39
00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.297
or apply what we call large language model
as judge.

40
00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:16.069
In order to evaluate the quality of this
more open-ended output of a text summary.

41
00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:19.539
Apart from testing and improving
the quality of your agent's output,

42
00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.442
you can also evaluate the path taken
by the agent

43
00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.879
to ensure it doesn't get stuck in a loop
or repeat steps unnecessarily.

44
00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:29.816
And so, in this course, you'll learn
how to structure your evaluations

45
00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:34.320
to iterate on and improve both the output
quality and the path taken by your agent.

46
00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:38.391
You'll do this by creating a code based
agent that operates as a data analyzer.

47
00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:39.826
The agent will have access

48
00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:43.696
to a set of tools that allow it to connect
to a database and perform analysis.

49
00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:47.800
A router that identifies
what tool to use, and a memory

50
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.235
that keeps track of the chat history.

51
00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:52.272
You'll
collect and evaluate traces of the steps

52
00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:56.075
taken by your agent to process
a query and visualize the collected data.

53
00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.144
You'll then learn how to evaluate

54
00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:01.614
each tool in your agent workflow
using different types of evaluators.

55
00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:06.186
You also evaluate if the router chooses
the right tool based on the user's query,

56
00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:09.389
and if it extracts the right parameters
to execute the tool

57
00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:12.592
and assess
the trajectory taken by the agent.

58
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:16.129
Finally, you put all of your evaluators
into a structured experiment

59
00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:19.165
that you can use to iterate on
and improve your agent.

60
00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:23.203
While the course focuses on
applying evaluation during development.

61
00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:26.906
You'll also learn how you can monitor
your agent during production.

62
00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:29.576
Many people have worked to create this
course.

63
